[{"key":"concurrency/sqlite-wal-concurrency","assignment":"concurrency","row":{"id":"sqlite-wal-concurrency","slot":"concurrency","finding":"SQLite WAL documents reader-writer overlap, but limits the database to one WAL writer at a time.","status":"documented_fact","support":[{"url":"https://www.sqlite.org/wal.html","title":"Write-Ahead Logging","passage":"WAL provides more concurrency as readers do not block writers and a writer does not block readers. Reading and writing can proceed concurrently.","locator":"Advantages and Disadvantages"},{"url":"https://www.sqlite.org/wal.html","title":"Write-Ahead Logging","passage":"Because writers do nothing that would interfere with the actions of readers, writers and readers can run at the same time. However, since there is only one WAL file, there can only be one writer at a time.","locator":"How WAL Works","origin":"SQLite official documentation","sourceType":"Primary documentation"}],"counterevidence":"The same page says reader/writer nonblocking is \"mostly true\" and identifies obscure cases in which a WAL-mode query can return SQLITE_BUSY.","method":"Applies only when SQLite is operating in WAL mode; this is documented locking behavior, not a throughput measurement.","comparability":"SQLite's single-WAL-writer constraint is not directly comparable to PostgreSQL without workload, transaction duration, isolation, storage, and contention measurements.","confidence":"High for the stated WAL behavior because both conditions are direct official documentation.","disposition":"retain"},"eligibleForReview":true,"receiptPath":"/home/balauru/.pi-profiles/fabric/tmp/fabric-research-rework-review/live/worker-concurrency.json","checked":false},{"key":"concurrency/postgresql-conflicting-writers","assignment":"concurrency","row":{"id":"postgresql-conflicting-writers","slot":"concurrency","finding":"PostgreSQL documents that a would-be updater encountering a concurrently updated, deleted, or locked target row waits for the first updater; at Repeatable Read, a committed competing update can roll back the transaction and require retry.","status":"documented_fact","support":[{"url":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/transaction-iso.html","title":"13.2. Transaction Isolation","passage":"However, such a target row might have already been updated (or deleted or locked) by another concurrent transaction by the time it is found. In this case, the would-be updater will wait for the first updating transaction to commit or roll back (if it is still in progress).","locator":"13.2.1 Read Committed Isolation Level","origin":"PostgreSQL official documentation","sourceType":"Primary documentation"},{"url":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/transaction-iso.html","title":"13.2. Transaction Isolation","passage":"Applications using this level must be prepared to retry transactions due to serialization failures.","locator":"13.2.2 Repeatable Read Isolation Level","origin":"PostgreSQL official documentation","sourceType":"Primary documentation"}],"counterevidence":"The retry statement is specific to Repeatable Read; the first passage describes the would-be updater behavior and does not establish a throughput outcome.","method":"Applies to the documented transaction isolation semantics and conflicts on a target row; no load test or throughput measurement was retrieved.","comparability":"Isolation level materially changes conflict handling. These passages cannot establish comparative writer throughput against SQLite WAL.","confidence":"High for the conflict and retry semantics within the named isolation contexts; limited for general reader-writer overlap because no exact MVCC passage was expanded.","disposition":"qualify"},"eligibleForReview":true,"receiptPath":"/home/balauru/.pi-profiles/fabric/tmp/fabric-research-rework-review/live/worker-concurrency.json","checked":false},{"key":"concurrency/deployment-topology","assignment":"concurrency","row":{"id":"deployment-topology","slot":"deployment","finding":"SQLite WAL requires all database processes on one host and does not work over a network filesystem; PostgreSQL's client-server protocol supports TCP/IP and Unix-domain sockets.","status":"documented_fact","support":[{"url":"https://www.sqlite.org/wal.html","title":"Write-Ahead Logging","passage":"All processes using a database must be on the same host computer; WAL does not work over a network filesystem. This is because WAL requires all processes to share a small amount of memory and processes on separate host machines obviously cannot share memory with each other.","locator":"Advantages and Disadvantages","origin":"SQLite official documentation","sourceType":"Primary documentation"},{"url":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/protocol.html","title":"Chapter 54. Frontend/Backend Protocol","passage":"PostgreSQL uses a message-based protocol for communication between frontends and backends (clients and servers). The protocol is supported over TCP/IP and also over Unix-domain sockets.","locator":"Chapter introduction; protocol version 3.2 introduced in PostgreSQL 18","origin":"PostgreSQL official documentation","sourceType":"Primary documentation"}],"method":"Documents supported connection and filesystem topology, not deployment effort, backup configuration, security posture, or performance.","comparability":"SQLite's condition is specifically WAL shared-memory/filesystem behavior; PostgreSQL's passage describes protocol transports. It does not alone compare operational complexity.","confidence":"High for the explicit topology statements; limited to the retrieved documentation scope.","disposition":"retain"},"eligibleForReview":true,"receiptPath":"/home/balauru/.pi-profiles/fabric/tmp/fabric-research-rework-review/live/worker-concurrency.json","checked":false},{"key":"backups/b1","assignment":"backups","row":{"id":"b1","slot":"backup","finding":"SQLite documents its Online Backup API as incremental copying in which the source is locked only during actual reads, rather than for the full copy.","status":"documented_fact","support":[{"url":"https://www.sqlite.org/backup.html","title":"SQLite Backup API","passage":"The online backup API allows the contents of one database to be copied into another database file, replacing any original contents of the target database. The copy operation may be done incrementally, in which case the source database does not need to be locked for the duration of the copy, only for the brief periods of time when it is actually being read from.","locator":"Online Backup API introduction","origin":"SQLite Documentation","sourceType":"Primary documentation"}],"method":"Current SQLite documentation URL retrieved. This supports API-level online-copy locking behavior, not a measured backup duration, throughput, or end-to-end restore reliability.","comparability":"Not directly comparable to PostgreSQL physical-backup requirements or performance; no matched workload measurement was retrieved.","confidence":"High for the stated API locking behavior because it is an exact primary-documentation passage.","disposition":"retain"},"eligibleForReview":true,"receiptPath":"/home/balauru/.pi-profiles/fabric/tmp/fabric-research-rework-review/live/worker-backups.json","checked":false},{"key":"backups/b2","assignment":"backups","row":{"id":"b2","slot":"backup","finding":"PostgreSQL documents pg_basebackup as an online physical cluster backup; a standalone result requires WAL inclusion, while continuous-archive recovery requires uninterrupted archived WAL reaching back to the backup start.","status":"documented_fact","support":[{"url":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/app-pgbasebackup.html","title":"pg_basebackup","passage":"pg_basebackup is used to take a base backup of a running PostgreSQL database cluster. The backup is taken without affecting other clients of the database, and can be used both for point-in-time recovery","locator":"Description","origin":"PostgreSQL Global Development Group documentation","sourceT